  "wiringNotes": [
   "Nest thermostat connectors are built so that only one wire can be inserted into each connector. Putting more than one wire into a Nest thermostat connector can damage your system and your thermostat. (answer/9250973)",
   "Do not enter jumper wires into the compatibility checker: 'If your thermostat has a short jumper wire between two connectors, don't include that wire. Only enter the labels for wires coming from inside your wall.' (answer/9246656)",
   "Ignore wire colors - enter the connector label, not the wire color: 'don't enter R in the Compatibility Checker if you find a red wire, or W if you find a white wire. The label on the wire connector is what you should enter.' (answer/9246656)",
   "Nest thermostats do not support stranded wire - they require solid-core copper wire, 18 to 22 gauge. 'Anything smaller than 22-gauge wire could be too thin for a Nest thermostat to detect.' (answer/9244860)",
   "High voltage systems (thermostat labeled 120 V or 240 V, two thick wires with wire nuts, e.g. electric baseboard) are not compatible; a pro can add a step-down transformer to 20-30 V. (answer/9246656)",
   "Star-terminal accessory wiring (humidifier, dehumidifier, multi-speed fan): 'If you've connected a humidifier, dehumidifier, or multi-speed fan wire to the star terminal, we strongly recommend that you get help from a pro.' (answer/9249351)",
   "Wire detection: 'Most Google Nest thermostats detect wires by testing them mechanically (if a wire is inserted into the connector) and electrically (electrical current on the wire).' Make sure the wire connector's button stays down - if not, the wire is not fully inserted and will not be detected. Exposed conductor should be 1/3 to 1/2 inch, clean and straight. (answer/9244860)",
   "Only one wire may be inserted per connector; putting more than one wire in a connector can damage the system and thermostat.",
   "Do not carry over old jumper wires from a prior thermostat - the 4th gen has an internal Rh/Rc jumper it uses automatically.",
   "If old thermostat has two wires in one labeled connector (e.g., two wires under 'W'), see the Google 'two labels' guidance before wiring.",
   "Stranded wire is not compatible. High-voltage systems (120V/240V, thick wires with wire nuts, or L1/L2 terminal blocks) are not compatible - do not connect.",
   "For dual-transformer systems (separate Rh and Rc from the wall) both wires can be landed in their respective Rh/Rc connectors - that's not a 'jumper' situation.",
   "Google's Home app compatibility checker generates a custom wiring diagram per household during setup; always follow that diagram over generic instructions.",
   "AQ+/AQ- replace the single star (*) terminal used on 3rd gen and earlier for accessory wiring; only insert a wire there if the app's wiring diagram calls for it.",
   "The 4th gen has no printed paper install guide in the box beyond a Welcome Guide - all install steps are delivered inside the Google Home app during setup (per the Welcome Guide PDF)."
  ],
  "setup": {
   "access": "On-thermostat: Quick View Menu > Settings > Equipment (shows detected wires, then lets you set Heat Pump O/B, etc). Newer generations are increasingly configured from the Google Home app: Home > Climate > your thermostat > Settings > Thermostat, scroll to the setting (e.g. Heat pump).",
   "keySettings": [
    {
     "setting": "Equipment / wiring detection",
     "options": "auto-detected from wires present",
     "notes": "Settings > Equipment shows every wire the thermostat detects (Home app: Settings > Thermostat Wiring)"
    },
    {
     "setting": "Heat pump reversing valve O/B",
     "options": "O or B",
     "notes": "Defaults to O; if wrong, heating/cooling will appear reversed. Confirm from the heat pump's own manual when possible. Settings > Equipment > Continue > Continue > Heat Pump on-device, or Home app Settings > Thermostat > Heat pump"
    },
    {
     "setting": "Reversing valve activation (heat pump)",
     "options": "Cyclical (energized during heat) or Standard/seasonal",
     "notes": "Terminology on 4th gen/newer is 'Cyclical'; 3rd gen and earlier call it 'Each cycle'/seasonal - wrong setting causes system to never shut off"
    },
    {
     "setting": "Emergency heat / AUX lockout temperature",
     "options": "outdoor temp threshold",
     "notes": "Below the AUX lockout temp, auxiliary heat is allowed to run with compressor"
    },
    {
     "setting": "Humidifier/dehumidifier/ventilator on AQ+/AQ-",
     "options": "select accessory type and 1-wire vs 2-wire",
     "notes": "App asks what is wired to AQ+ (and AQ- if used) during setup"
    },
    {
     "setting": "Temperature scale",
     "options": "Fahrenheit or Celsius",
     "notes": ""
    },
    {
     "setting": "Lock / range lock",
     "options": "on/off with 4-digit PIN, min range 5 deg F (3 deg C)",
     "notes": "Settings > Lock in app; unlock with PIN on thermostat or in app"
    },
    {
     "setting": "System Test",
     "options": "Heating / Cooling / Fan",
     "notes": "Runs each stage for verification; see installerTest"
    }
   ],
   "installerTest": "SYSTEM TEST (answer/9225726). During setup: 1) Choose 'Test Now' once it shows on the thermostat screen. 2) Choose a specific system function to test - Heating, Cooling, or Fan (options your system does not have are not shown); select Continue to go back to the Equipment menu. 3) The thermostat tells you what to look for, e.g. 'Radiators should be warming up' - go verify it. 4) Select 'Continue Testing' for systems slow to warm/cool (in-floor radiant can need 30 minutes or more). 5) Select Done if the system responds correctly, or Troubleshoot if there is a problem. If the display shows a code beginning with T (such as T03), use the Nest thermostat help codes article. AFTER SETUP, for the Nest Thermostat and Nest Learning Thermostat (4th gen): Open the Home app > tap Favorites or Devices > tap your thermostat > select Mode > pick Cool, Heat, or Fan > for heating turn the target temperature up until it turns orange, for cooling turn it down until it turns blue > wait about 5 minutes and check the air from the vents/radiators (30+ minutes for slow systems). NOTE: 'If it's too hot or cold to test your system, your thermostat may prevent you from testing a mode. This is to help prevent damage to your system.'",
   "factoryReset": "Google Home app: Home > Climate > your thermostat > Settings > Factory Reset. After the reset completes, go to the Home tab, tap Add > Device, and scan the device QR code (or tap Use setup code / Add a different way), then re-enter any wires connected to your thermostat. (answer/9244860)"
  },
